# dominance heirarchy

Lucezia tied herself to the main mast of their improvized flying stage-ship. The events of the first act cover a full diurnal cycle, or at least focus on them. 

There had been a moment when Spavento, squat and bristling, and Pandolfo, broad and impassive, might have tried to kill each other. Some killer impulse, like dogs over food.

This is not a society of heavily policed gender norms, not like yours or the author's at the time of writing, and certainly not like a similar historical period in your own past.

Say that it is due to telepathy, or just the author's preference, in this volume at least, to focus on questions of class and economic power, rather than race or gender.

Eventually, this can be implicit in the way the characters feel and act towards each other, but I want to state it as an intention now, and am looking for feedback as to how to avoid flattening out the world and character, and to use more equal gender relationships as a means of speculation, not objectification or fantasy. 



