#cat 

Lucrezia heard a mewling and scratching behind the crate, where its skid of anti-gravity had caused it to shift on a lumped scroll of replacement canvas. Pulling back a corner where Jacques had used calligrapher's ink and wildflowers to sketch a quick backdrop for a puppet show (an orchard of gravitic apples and pecans, furrowing in washed ripples that weather had seasoned from bright grasses to grey husks), she found the green eyes and gleaming teeth of a kitten. The same, no doubt, that Spavento had startled earlier. 

Her eyes welled with tears. 

This series of novels, over time, has come to represent, for the author, or illustrate, ideas about how humans can collaborate, and how narrative can leverage the hyperlocal and contextual to tell stories impossible outside of a given community or audience.

One of these ideas is that fiction can be useful. The author doesn't want to create knock-off versions of commercial products or property. 

She grabbed the kitten expertly by the scruff, and let it bite her hand, breaking skin but relenting before it went too deep. A moment of the awkward intimacy of violence, and then it let her scratch the scruff of its neck, bury her face in its fur. She felt, or imagined, a flea jump from the smaller host to a larger. 

This project, and its eventual readers and collaborators, could be a working metaphor for the ramshackle stage-ship drifting over battlefields. Or vice versa. But the author wants to make the unfair move of using this little kitten as one, really the only, direct link or correlation between the world of these novels and his. 

There is a kitten, and he has to write about it to a committee.

Pandolfo, she saw, was staring at her, the corners of his lips turned up in what might have been a smile. He stood, the plane of the raft shifting with displaced weight as he reached out, not to touch her as she thought at first, but, after she had already cringed away, to scratch the kitten behind its ears. Unsurprisingly, the animal took to him immediately, purring and closing its eyes.

He did touch her then, gripping her shoulder gently, and she leaned into him by some immesurable degree, that might have just been the shifting planks. Graziano, Lucrezia also noticed, was watching the exchange. 

Lombarqo was the only one that slept, having secured himself, despite his drunkenness, with the professional discipline of one who had battled to exhaustion many times on flying castles, grabbing sleep while distant screams meant the battle line was far enough away that death was, for the space of troubled dreams, a distance less than immanent.

It wasn't Lombarqo's thoughts, she realized, but actual screams below them. The two watchful men had heard it was well. Luckily, the sporadic fires they had seen to the East hadn't spread.

"What do you think it is?" She asked the bishop.

He shrugged. "Battle lines shifting again. As long as we're up here it doesn't matter. Being seen by anyone would be a mistake."

The arrangement of the sails had been another source of tension between Graziano and Pandolfo. They had needed to have the sails set in such a way that they could functionally catch wind and change their direction, but that would not appear deliberate, betraying them as anything other than another bit of mangled scaffolding of war falling from a ziggourat.

She was the one who knew how the poles and canvas sheets needed to be arranged, having watched her family solve similar problems of letitation in the past, but she lacked the physical strength to do it herself, as well as the patience necessary to get them to cooperate.

"If you are going to kill each other, do it now," Lucrezia said. "Otherwise help me tie this off."

"We need their weight," Graziano had pointed out, wearing a ridiculous runed robe from the costume chest, belted against the alpine chill. "So make sure not to drop the corpse. We can hang it from below."

"We'd loose too much to the birds," Spavento had growled, grinning.

The subtle boundary between jest and plea.

