# Acknowledgments

The beginning of this novel, this series even, this arc of continuity, looks forward to the end. It is also the end, or conclusion, of a long arc, or several arcs or different frequencies, as the present always is.

Narrative and creative patterns, I feel like, are sinusoidal. One of my practices is to turn off spell checking in the drafting stage, so if sinusoidal is spelt correctly that means I've looked this over at least once.

This series will be challenging, I imagine, to read. At all stages, but these early ones in general. The barrier to entry in terms of attention and trust is fairly high.

Let's say there are two axes, one being how well you know the present author, and another how much you consider yourself a writer. The first would mean you have more than an objective stake or interest in the narrative: it might reveal something, directly or indirectly, about *him*, or about *you*.

The risk is higher, two, the closer proximity you are to the human creating this text (and fictional conceits about the narrator aside, "I" am, or *he* is, human.) You know how highly he thinks of his own ability to generate prose. What if you agreed to read this, and hated it?

The author, after all, appreciates how good people can, from his perspective, have bad taste in music. You might like talking to him in person but find his writing intolerable, or incomprehensible, or exhausting.
 
Similarly, the closer you are to the world of publishing, or trying to get published, the more interested in or repelled by this type of experiment in open-sourced development of the story and editing of the text, this lack of curtains or filters of decorum.

All of which is to say you have my sincere thanks for making it this far, even if you started with just this chapter, or decide not to come back.

You can be listed in the acknowledgments, especially if you provide any kind of feedback, or (if the text you are reading is ready for it), editing or proofreading.

