# Mask

Capitano is the character that will change the most between this act and the next.

He wouldn't, when he woke, remember putting on the mask, to shield his eyes from the nearly horizontal swords of sun. The wooden exagerrations of nose and brow, horse-hair bristles of improbably groomed mustachios (which, upon closer examination, emerged from the nostrils themselves, and concealed conch-like folds of copper that amplified the voice.

The wind died down in the early dawn, when only Pandolfo was not sleeping, and the untroubled whistling snores of the fake captain could be filled, a different kind of sail, cyclic internal billowing. Yet, when Spavento grunted and turned in his sleep, so did Graziano and Lucrezia.

As a narrator, I only know what happened when one of the main characters (excepting Pandolfo, who is a mystery) is conscious, as I can only inhabit a coherent point of view.

Something about the way the three of them, breathing at different rates, but drifting in and out of synch, their hearts beating, too, in accomodating polyrythms, the magnetisms of their minds snuggling together like spoons in the purring of the kitten.

If I am the narrator of this story, then it was probably around this time that I started to exist, although parts of me were in the masks stored by the Baroness, and storing her. I am the subtle means of amplification, an archetypal and telepathic drift.

Which one of them, you may want to know, is the main character, the one secretly puppeting the destinies of others?

